{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/821865-affecting-dreams","id":821865,"title":"Affecting Dreams","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-02-02T14:01:36-08:00","id":821865,"title":"Affecting Dreams","content":"Hi all.<br /> <br /> I'm currently gearing up to run a VTR 2nd Edition game, and getting my house rules in order. There's a few things I want to handle a bit different to regular VTR.<br /> <br /> One of those areas - pretty minor one, to be frank - is vampire powers that affect dreams. I'd like to allow for the possibility, but not in a way that is so narrowly focused as the Alucinor bloodline or custom bloodline-only disciplines generally. The Alucinor devotion Sleepwalker is a great example of the sort of thing I believe I need - just a devotion or two for the vampire who develops that way. Or, really, just an idea of how I'd implement such devotions if it becomes necessary, because I don't yet know whether it will be needed, but I do think &quot;once bitten, her dreams were plagued by thoughts of the vampyre&quot; is a reasonably common trope of the fiction.<br /> <br /> So, the real question... Which discipline best covers dreams, for the purpose of building devotions affecting dreams?<br /> <br /> Wet Dreams is Majesty 2, Nightmare 2, indicating that Nightmare is appropriate. Sleepwalker is Dominate 2, Insomnium 3, but as noted I'd rather not use Insomnium (and possibly use a re-implementation of Insomnium powers as devotions, which leads back into the question).<br /> <br /> So, is Nightmare the discipline of choice here, or does someone have an argument in favour of Dominate (the discipline of the mind) or even Auspex (which covers telepathy via Lay Open the Mind)? Or is a new discipline necessary?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","name":"travisjhall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-02T14:13:02-08:00","id":821871,"title":"","content":"Mythologies has a dream eating devotion and other mechanics in it, starting on page 73, it's a 1e book but might give you some inspiration and a few mechanics to toy with. It uses Auspex 1, and Dominate or Majesty 1. It allows the Vampire 'drinking' the dream to experience it as it happens, and then 'replay' it in their mind later, as well as gaining a +1 to social rolls, and the chance to be eaten by a dream entity if one exists in that dream.<br /> <br /> Another 1e resource, Auspex 4 (no devotion needed), according to Astral Realms of all places, allows a Vampire to not only read a dream as it's occurring, but to more or less 'piggy back' in it, appearing in it, though gaining no special ways to manipulate it further. This also allows minor astral travel, but that's irrelevant to what you're asking. Since Auspex 4 is so similar to 1e's, I'd allow this to work as is, but use the Dream Form mechanics in Beast (and eventually Changeling/Mage will have it too) for your attributes in the dream.<br /> <br /> As for further devotion creation, Auspex seems appropriate for most dream travel or information gathering from dreams. I'd say both Dominate and Nightmare could be used for devotions meant to manipulate their contents, depending on the intent and flavor of the devotion.<br /> <br /> (I've also redone Alucinor for 2e, and you can find that in my homebrew hub, but you said you don't exactly want to rely on that, so that's all i'll say on that)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/themes/stvault/images/affiliatebanner4.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Christopher%20Falco&amp;affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\">Storytellers' Vault supplements (WoD, CoD)</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/FalconianP\" target=\"_blank\">My Patreon! (CofD bonuses include: Fae Sorcery/Technomancy for Vampires, Quick Conversion for Inferno, and more!)</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1130-falco1029","name":"falco1029","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1130_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1045"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-02T17:18:15-08:00","id":821994,"title":"","content":"Hi Falco. I have found your re-implementation of the Alucinor (and I note that you gave them Nightmare as a bloodline discipline - relevant data there), but the issue there is that bloodlines strongly focus a character on a concept and particular set of powers, which doesn't much help for the character with just a smattering of power over dreams - the character who just calls his victims to him in their sleep without actually invading dreams, for example. This issue isn't anything to do with your work on the bloodline, but has to do with the suitability of bloodlines to implement certain characters generally. Sometimes they work well, sometimes another approach is better. But I may further adapt the devotions you gave for your version of the Alucinor - it's good fodder for inspiration.<br /> <br /> The Dream-Drinking devotion from Mythologies is Auspex 1, Dominate or Majesty 1, so I guess that's Auspex because you're discovering information and Dominate or Majesty for the mental connection.<br /> <br /> I guess the evidence points to dreams falling under a variety of disciplines depending on what you're doing with them - Auspex to watch passively, Dominate, Nightmare and maybe even Majesty to alter.<br /> <br /> Something I should note is that I don't have to worry about power interactions with Changelings or even Mages. I do love Lost especially, and fully intend on running a Lost game at some stage, but in order to avoid diluting the themes of my Vampire game I've simply declared that Changelings and their like will not be appearing in this game. Similarly Mages won't appear (though I have said that there may be mortal sorcerers - thaumaturges a la Second Sight, for example).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","name":"travisjhall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-03T14:23:35-08:00","id":822758,"title":"","content":"So, new sub-question... If reimplementing the Sleepwalker devotion for a game in which Insomnium doesn't exist, what would the discipline requirements be?<br /> <br /> Just calling it Dominate 2 seems a little low.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","name":"travisjhall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-03T14:28:21-08:00","id":822763,"title":"","content":"It doesn't need one. You can use Dominate 3 to accomplish sleepwalker by making the trigger 'the next time you sleep'.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/themes/stvault/images/affiliatebanner4.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Christopher%20Falco&amp;affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\">Storytellers' Vault supplements (WoD, CoD)</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/FalconianP\" target=\"_blank\">My Patreon! (CofD bonuses include: Fae Sorcery/Technomancy for Vampires, Quick Conversion for Inferno, and more!)</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1130-falco1029","name":"falco1029","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1130_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1045"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-03T16:00:54-08:00","id":822841,"title":"","content":"I guess, if you want to say that anybody's subconscious is capable of taking that person sleepwalking. Normally the brain has mechanisms to prevent that; most people aren't capable of sleepwalking even if they want to. (It's recognised as a disorder, and even seems to be inheritable.) That's why the Sleepwalker devotion existed in the first place; it allowed the vampire to inflict somnambulism on the victim, as well as deliver a command.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I hadn't realised that the instruction to sleepwalk had to be implanted in advance (which means actually invading a dream to give the instruction isn't necessary). That makes it pretty close to straight Dominate 3.<br /> <br /> But it certainly also leaves open the possibility of another devotion that does allow a vampire to summon a sleeper. That'd be a variant on the Summoning devotion, I guess. Or, an Auspex 4 devotion that simply allows Lay Open the Mind to replace the need for eye contact for use of Dominate (if you don't want to rule that Lay Open the Mind does that by itself).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","name":"travisjhall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-03T16:13:05-08:00","id":822850,"title":"","content":"I'd personally say it is. If a given ST doesn't want to allow that, I'd say a Dominate 3 devotion could simply add that capability, making someone into a Sleepwalker that can be commanded without waking them up. That could then perhaps be used to set someone up to be Summoned later if you have that devotion.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/themes/stvault/images/affiliatebanner4.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Christopher%20Falco&amp;affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\">Storytellers' Vault supplements (WoD, CoD)</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/FalconianP\" target=\"_blank\">My Patreon! (CofD bonuses include: Fae Sorcery/Technomancy for Vampires, Quick Conversion for Inferno, and more!)</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1130-falco1029","name":"falco1029","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1130_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1045"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T05:40:59-08:00","id":834967,"title":"","content":"Sorry if this is written anywhere, but I didn't find it...<br /> Do Kindred without Dream Visions dream?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MAKE LOVE, NOT SPAM!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1956-the-boz","name":"The Boz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1956_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T06:35:56-08:00","id":834997,"title":"","content":"In Torpor, yes. The dreams help them acclimate to whatever time period they wake up in. No idea about daysleep.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T07:16:39-08:00","id":835019,"title":"","content":"Daysleep is what I'm asking, sorry for not specifying.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MAKE LOVE, NOT SPAM!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1956-the-boz","name":"The Boz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1956_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-22T09:05:57-08:00","id":835094,"title":"","content":"Yes, they do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?affiliate_id=259490\" t